phoma** (as a primary modality alone or with other therapies), and **chordoma**. It may be used **instead of surgery** when surgery cannot be performed, **post‑operatively (adjuvant)** when concerning features or residual disease remain, as **consolidation** to eliminate remaining cancer cells, and in some settings to directly treat **metastatic sites** or provide **palliation** for symptomatic tumors.\n\n**RADIATION THERAPY** is frequently combined with systemic treatments, particularly **chemotherapy** (chemoradiation), including concurrent regimens in anal cancer and situations where platinum chemotherapy is paired with radiation. Prior radiation exposure to an area can influence later treatment choices; for example, previous neck radiation may affect surgical recommendations, prior radiation can make subsequent cutaneous squamous cell cancer higher risk, and radiation may not be reusable in the same field for some recurrences (though select exceptions are noted, such as certain inguinal lymph node recurrences when no prior radiation was given).\n\nSide effects depend on the treated region. Pelvic radiation can cause **vaginal tissue changes** (shortening, narrowing/stenosis) and may **weaken pelvic bones**; radiation can also **damage ovaries or testicles**, impair **fertility**, and may prompt fertility preservation strategies (e.g., ovarian transposition). Head and neck radiation can affect endocrine glands (thyroid/pituitary), supporting **endocrine monitoring**. In chemoradiation contexts, radiation may weaken or damage tissues such as the **perineum**, potentially impairing wound healing after procedures like APR. Radiation can also be used for **ovarian ablation** to permanently stop ovarian hormone production. Practical considerations include targeting decisions, number of sessions (including shorter courses), short‑ and long‑term side effects, strategies to minimize toxicity, and household safety steps after certain types of radiation.", "role": "assista